WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.986
Page organization affects how well a Razor
Pages application can be reviewed.

2
00:00:08.066 --> 00:00:12.944
A small application may begin with only a
few pages,

3
00:00:13.024 --> 00:00:19.376
but it can grow into public pages, account
pages, user dashboards,

4
00:00:19.456 --> 00:00:26.715
administrative workflows, support tools,
reporting pages, upload pages,

5
00:00:26.795 --> 00:00:29.064
and operational views.

6
00:00:29.144 --> 00:00:35.042
When those areas are mixed together
without clear structure,

7
00:00:35.122 --> 00:00:39.659
access expectations become harder to
inspect.

8
00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:45.524
Page models and handler methods should
communicate intent.

9
00:00:45.604 --> 00:00:53.317
A handler that only renders a view has
different expectations from a handler that

10
00:00:53.397 --> 00:01:00.429
changes account settings, updates a
workflow, modifies configuration,

11
00:01:00.509 --> 00:01:02.778
or records an approval.

12
00:01:02.858 --> 00:01:06.147
Handler names, folder placement,

13
00:01:06.227 --> 00:01:14.054
and page model structure should help
reviewers identify state-changing actions

14
00:01:14.134 --> 00:01:19.352
quickly, especially in pages with multiple
handlers.

15
00:01:19.432 --> 00:01:23.402
Folder structure is a useful review tool.

16
00:01:23.482 --> 00:01:30.174
Public pages, authenticated user
workflows, administrative pages,

17
00:01:30.254 --> 00:01:38.080
sensitive data workflows, and operational
pages should be separated in ways that

18
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.904
match the application's access model.

19
00:01:41.984 --> 00:01:49.810
Folder-level conventions can then support
consistent authorization and reduce

20
00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.272
one-off page decisions.

21
00:01:52.352 --> 00:01:58.024
Routing and friendly URLs should help
users and reviewers,

22
00:01:58.104 --> 00:02:02.414
but they should not hide sensitive
behavior.

23
00:02:02.494 --> 00:02:10.434
A friendly route can make an application
feel clean while still leading to a handler

24
00:02:10.514 --> 00:02:13.349
that changes important data.

25
00:02:13.429 --> 00:02:21.369
Review should follow the page model and
handler behavior, not only the visible URL.

26
00:02:21.449 --> 00:02:25.760
Shared layouts, partial views, tag
helpers,

27
00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.377
and page conventions can improve
consistency.

28
00:02:30.457 --> 00:02:36.809
They can also hide exceptions if teams
rely on them without review.

29
00:02:36.889 --> 00:02:44.488
Shared elements are helpful when they make
repeated decisions easier to apply,

30
00:02:44.568 --> 00:02:50.353
but a page with unusual access rules,
unusual form behavior,

31
00:02:50.433 --> 00:02:55.538
or sensitive data still deserves direct
inspection.

32
00:02:55.618 --> 00:03:02.764
A practical review asks four questions for
each page area: who can reach it,

33
00:03:02.844 --> 00:03:10.443
what state can change, what request data
is accepted, and what data is returned.

34
00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:15.741
If the page organization makes those
answers obvious,

35
00:03:15.821 --> 00:03:22.740
Razor Pages becomes easier to maintain
safely as the application grows.
